      "id": 1,
      "text": "Before white settlers arrived in the 1830s, the Potawatomi people camped permanently along the Grand River in what is now Dimondale.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        8
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dimondale%2C_Michigan",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "A post office called East Windsor opened in 1860 and was moved to Dimondale, where it was renamed in 1872.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2,
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dimondale%2C_Michigan",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The Lake Shore and Michigan Southern Railway arrived in 1878, establishing a station that gave the area its name, Dimondale.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        12
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dimondale%2C_Michigan",
      "category": "transport",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Dimondale officially became a village in 1906, formalizing its local governance.",
